WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.040
If you use coding

2
00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:09.160
agents, you've been hearing a lot about context management,

3
00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:14.760
and you've probably noticed how tedious and imprecise it can be.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.120
And that's because really, it feels like more of an art than a science.

5
00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.800
These days, we're kind of just sharing around markdown documents.

6
00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.240
We're not really checking to see if they improve our agent's behaviour or not.

7
00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.080
It's very vibes based, and that's okay, because the ecosystem

8
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:32.880
is moving really quickly.

9
00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.040
But I think it's time that we start treating this context

10
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.640
as a first class primitive in our software and in our processes.

11
00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.080
And that's really where Tessl comes in.

12
00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:48.960
So Tessl allows you to version context, to share context

13
00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:53.520
and to evaluate it to see if it's actually improving agent behaviour.

14
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.440
So let's talk about how we eval your context.

15
00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.440
We do three types of evals in the product today.

16
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.240
Task evals, skill review and repo evals.

17
00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:11.240
Task evals are about evaluating agent behaviour with and without your context.

18
00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.560
So we develop scenarios based on your context

19
00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:17.920
and then see how the agent behaves with and without it.

20
00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:22.000
This is really not about the content of your context.

21
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.160
It's about how it affects agent behaviour.

22
00:01:26.600 --> 00:01:27.880
Skill review.

23
00:01:27.880 --> 00:01:30.440
It's a little cheeky to call this an eval.

24
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.640
I think of this more as skill linting.

25
00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.920
So we've encoded a bunch of skill writing

26
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:40.360
best practices from Anthropic and other sources.

27
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.920
And we're just evaluating the actual content of your skill

28
00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:49.320
for how well it's written and how well it conforms to those best practices.

29
00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:53.920
So for repo evals we are generating

30
00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.400
realistic scenarios based on recent commit history.

31
00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:02.680
And we're seeing how your agent fares in that environment

32
00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:05.800
with and without your context and scoring the results.

33
00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:06.800
Of course.

34
00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:12.040
You know, if you've run any type of eval before that there's variance to deal with.

35
00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:15.760
LLMs and agent behaviour are non-deterministic.

36
00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.240
So we've done a lot of the work for you around normalising

37
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.240
those scores excluding crazy outliers.

38
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.360
Really we've done a lot of the thinking around

39
00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.760
how to make this as easy as possible Evals are important

40
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.760
because otherwise context is just markdown.

41
00:02:30.920 --> 00:02:35.040
You can get started for free at tessl.io/registry.
